WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:05.960
Logan is a masterful piece of cinema and a
beautiful send-off for Hugh Jackman's Wolverine.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:10.820
Still, some interesting scenes and ideas ended
up cut from the final movie including a reunion

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:14.269
with Sabretooth, mutant cameos, and a tragic
flashback.

00:00:14.269 --> 00:00:17.640
Yippee-ki-yay movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and in this video I'm going to talk about

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:21.160
some of the scenes and concepts rejected or
cut from the final Logan movie.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:26.200
Just before I get into those, I've got a cool
giveaway for a second set of Logan merchandise.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:30.260
For a chance to win, all you have to do is
subscribe and leave a comment about Logan

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.140
or the deleted scenes on this video.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:37.289
The prize includes a T-shirt, shot glasses,
a dog-tag bottle opener and a metal notebook.

00:00:37.289 --> 00:00:41.289
And for bonus ways to enter, check out the
Gleam link in the video description below.

00:00:41.289 --> 00:00:45.170
Of course, there are spoilers in this video,
so if you haven't seen Logan yet check out

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:49.410
my spoiler-free review and come back here
when you've had a chance to watch the movie!

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:53.550
An idea that was rejected early in script
development was a flashback scene that would

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:56.969
have shown exactly what happened during the
Westchester incident.

00:00:56.969 --> 00:01:01.609
In the final movie, we get various allusions
to the catastrophe, such a news report stating

00:01:01.609 --> 00:01:05.479
that an incident similar to the one we see
at the casino in Oklahoma City occurred about

00:01:05.479 --> 00:01:10.090
a year previously and that 7 mutants were
killed and over 600 people were injured.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:13.920
The original idea was that the flashback would
explain what happened with Professor X and

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.250
Wolverine in Westchester and it was actually
going to be the very first scene in the movie;

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:22.950
however, the filmmakers thought it would be
more powerful if the audience was left wondering.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:26.640
Screenwriter Michael Green says he's interested
to see what fans theorise about the event,

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.950
but that knowing exactly what happened doesn't
matter; it's the "emotional effect" that the

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:34.560
incident had on Logan and Charles that's important.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.390
Flashbacks were also considered for other
moments in Logan's past, for example, there's

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:43.159
some beautiful unused concept art of Alkali
Lake by designer Shae Shatz.

00:01:43.159 --> 00:01:46.950
And there was another idea for a flashback
scene featuring the location where the movie's

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:51.579
villains lived, a residence which included
a pool, a helipad, and a killer view.

00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:56.210
The unused concept art also reveals an alternate
design for The Tank where Charles was being

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:57.610
kept by Logan.

00:01:57.610 --> 00:02:02.400
And curiously, in some early sketches, it
looks like Logan originally had a little canine

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:06.479
companion which might have given him more
of a Mad Max vibe.

00:02:06.479 --> 00:02:10.399
And it was in Oklahoma City where we might
have seen a reunion between Logan and Victor

00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:12.360
Creed, aka Sabretooth.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:16.230
As the Logan movie was being developed, Liev
Schreiber expressed interest in reprising

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:20.510
the role of Sabretooth one final time, and
an early idea was mooted that Logan would

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:23.720
seek out his half-brother and former nemesis
Sabretooth for help.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:26.900
It was never to be though and the idea fell
by the wayside.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:30.849
But would you have liked to have seen the
pair back together before Hugh Jackman's retirement

00:02:30.849 --> 00:02:31.849
as Wolverine?

00:02:31.849 --> 00:02:36.180
I've gotta say I loved the tone they went
for in the movie and various mutant cameos

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.230
might not have fit so well with that pared
down story.

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:43.900
Still, although the Wolverine clone X-24 was
played by Hugh Jackman himself, I did get

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:49.000
a slight Sabretooth vibe when X-24 first appeared
during that brutal fight scene at the Munson

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:50.000
family home.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.120
The dinner conversation that Logan and Charles
have with the Munsons could have been much

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:58.190
darker as an alternate version of the scene
was filmed where the Munsons ask Logan if

00:02:58.190 --> 00:02:59.190
he's married.

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:02.549
Charles replies that Logan was married, but
that he killed her.

00:03:02.549 --> 00:03:05.780
Mangold explained that he didn't use that
version of the scene because it just felt

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:10.190
too dark at a moment in the film that was
meant to provide a little levity, especially

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:12.569
with the events which happen later at the
Munson's farm.

00:03:12.569 --> 00:03:17.299
By the way, if that scene had made it into
the final film, it's unclear what exactly

00:03:17.299 --> 00:03:21.629
that would have meant for the X-Men timeline
which, after Days of Future Past, appeared

00:03:21.629 --> 00:03:25.879
to invalidate the timeline of the first three
X-Men movies at the end of which Wolverine

00:03:25.879 --> 00:03:27.310
killed Jean Grey.

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:31.910
Early in the story, director James Mangold
also toyed with the idea of Logan meeting

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:35.659
a group of other mutants on an underground
railroad during his journey.

00:03:35.659 --> 00:03:40.140
The mutants would probably have been cameos
by characters that fans would recognise, however,

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.860
Mangold decided that including them would
have felt forced given the movie's tone and,

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:47.550
ultimately, they would have undermined "the
loneliness of" Logan's world.

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:51.340
One character that fans have been desperate
to see alongside Wolverine and, for a long

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:53.269
time, was rumoured to appear in Logan is Deadpool.

00:03:53.269 --> 00:03:56.909
It would have been great to see Wolverine
and Deadpool on screen together, you know,

00:03:56.909 --> 00:04:02.502
in a good film, not just X-Men Origins: Wolverine,
but as Ryan Reynolds explained the Merc with

00:04:02.502 --> 00:04:05.150
a Mouth wouldn't have mixed with the tone
of Logan.

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:09.210
Wade Wilson and Deadpool did get a little
teaser for Deadpool 2, complete with a cameo

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:13.709
by Stan Lee, in the short "No Good Deed",
but given the way the movie Logan worked out

00:04:13.709 --> 00:04:18.080
in terms of mood and storyline, for me, it
was better that Deadpool wasn't part of the

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:19.549
main film.

00:04:19.549 --> 00:04:24.110
The supervillain Mr Sinister, aka Nathaniel
Essex, was teased in the X-Men: Apocalypse

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:28.060
post-credits, with Bryan Singer stating that
the third Wolverine movie would introduce

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:29.190
the villain properly.

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:34.020
However, later James Mangold decided not to
include the flashy supervillain in Logan as

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:37.990
he wanted to keep the film very grounded and
a step away from the huge spectacle of the

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:40.930
other movies in the X-Men franchise.

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:44.130
Mangold wanted the audience to feel as if
they could "run into Wolverine" on the street,

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:48.870
so he made the choice to avoid including larger-than-life
villains in brightly-coloured costumes like

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:50.030
Mr Sinister.

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:53.949
But don't despair if you're a fan of the Mr
Sinister character as Simon Kinberg has now

00:04:53.949 --> 00:04:58.220
said that Mr Sinister will be introduced in
a future X-Men movie.

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:01.919
It was certainly a great choice to make Logan
a very different type of movie within the

00:05:01.919 --> 00:05:06.940
superhero genre, however it does mean we never
got to see Hugh Jackman in Wolverine's classic

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:08.520
canary yellow costume.

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:14.220
We did see the suit once, but only in an alternate
ending to Mangold's 2013 The Wolverine movie.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:18.530
Actually, before Logan was released, Hugh
Jackman teased fans when he tweeted a picture

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:21.280
of a Wolverine action figure in the original
costume.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:25.150
The bright yellow costume wouldn't have fit
in neatly with the tone that the Logan movie

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:29.000
went for though, so in the end the closest
we got was via an easter egg to it at Logan's

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:33.310
funeral when one of the mutant children was
holding a Wolverine toy dressed in the classic

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:34.460
costume.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:38.310
Mangold has explained his decision not to
include the costume by stating that, for him,

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:42.490
"Logan is the least narcissistic of all the
superheroes" and that "the desire to put on

00:05:42.490 --> 00:05:46.210
a trademarked outfit ... and ... prance about
doing good deeds'" while people recognise

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:48.210
him for them seems very unlike Wolverine.

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:52.910
In fact, in the final movie, Logan himself
tears in to the way events from his life are

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:56.840
depicted in the comics and has no time for
'self-promoting' people in leotards.

00:05:56.840 --> 00:06:01.639
As for Mangold, he believes that Wolverine's
costume works best in the comics, but not

00:06:01.639 --> 00:06:05.380
so much on screen, or at least not on Hugh
Jackman in the Logan movie.

